1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:16,600
HOST: North America in 1491 was not one society waiting to be discovered.  Millions of people lived in distinct communities, spoke many languages, and built economies adapted to local environments. The useful starting point is diversity, not a single stereotype.

2
00:00:16,600 --> 00:00:37,200
HOST: By the end of this lesson, you will be able to do three things. One: identify how climate and resources shaped where people settled and how they lived. Two: compare how societies organized themselves in different regions. Three: explain why North America in 1491 was not one single culture.

3
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:52,200
HOST: Near the Mississippi River,  Cahokia became a large urban center supported by maize agriculture. Its monumental earthworks and far-reaching trade connections demonstrate organized labor, political authority, and exchange networks that linked communities across long distances.

4
00:00:52,200 --> 00:01:47,599
HOST: Travel southwest, and the problem flips. Around present-day New Mexico and Arizona, rain is scarce and rivers run only part of the year.  The Pueblo peoples answered with engineering. They built irrigation canals that carried river water to fields of maize, beans, and squash, the same three crops Cahokia grew, coaxed from a desert. And they built upward: multistory villages of adobe, like Taos Pueblo, where hundreds of people lived in connected rooms around a central plaza. Village life ran on cooperation: maintaining canals took organized labor, and clan networks shared both the work and the harvests. Underground kivas anchored ceremonial life, and the central plaza hosted the dances and markets that bound each town together. Dry country did not mean scattered country. Careful water management made permanent towns possible where rainfall alone never could have.

5
00:01:47,599 --> 00:02:34,199
HOST: Now head northeast, to the woodlands of present-day New York. Here the Haudenosaunee, called the Iroquois by the French, built something rare in world history: a lasting alliance.  Five nations, Mohawk, Oneida, Onondaga, Cayuga, and Seneca, bound themselves under the Great Law of Peace. Decisions required consensus, not majority rule. Fifty sachems, chosen by clan mothers, met in council and talked until they agreed. There was no king and no emperor. There was agreement. Villages of longhouses, each housing several related families, farmed maize, beans, and squash in forest clearings while men hunted and fished. When Europeans arrived, this confederacy would bargain with Dutch, French, and English as a power in its own right.

6
00:02:34,199 --> 00:02:52,799
HOST: Different environments encouraged different strategies.  Agricultural communities in the Southwest managed scarce water and built permanent settlements. On the Great Plains, many communities combined farming, hunting, and trade; later access to horses would change Plains life further. Environment influenced societies without mechanically determining them.

7
00:02:52,799 --> 00:03:08,599
HOST: For an A-P-U-S-H argument, connect environment to evidence. Geography shaped available resources; communities made choices about agriculture, settlement, and exchange;[/fast] those choices produced regional diversity. Avoid treating Indigenous peoples as a single group or as passive actors.[/slow]
